Yep. Utter is richer than it looks.

As a verb, utter means to give expression to something—to speak or emit it. It traces through Middle English and ultimately Germanic roots associated with putting something out.

As an adjective, though, utter means complete, absolute, total: utter silence, utter chaos.

So there’s a neat semantic tension:

utter = put outward
utter = all the way / complete

That makes utterance interesting in the frame we were just building. An internal meaning-space may be high-dimensional, contextual, relational, even partly nonverbal. To utter it, you have to project some portion outward into a transmissible representation.

M_{\text{internal}}\xrightarrow{\text{utter}}S_{\text{external}}

But S isn’t necessarily the utter M—the complete meaning. 😏

Then the receiver performs the inverse problem:

S+\text{context}+\text{decoder}\rightarrow \hat M

Which lands directly on what you just said:

I can hear exactly what you uttered without yet knowing utterly what you meant.

That’s a good little semantic fossil.